k I have to  tell you these are vital issues but you  know I can't sit here and say that I'm  in a climate activists that I've been  pushing the envelope of these issues  myself why is that you know we all have  grown up into a world and develop  different kinds of interests and  expertise and and it's very hard to  shift away from that right my footprint  is in mathematics and in physics and in  in communicating those ideas to a wider  audience and those things are important  too so it's trying to find a way that  everyone is so deeply invested in the  cause and can contribute in a way that's  somehow iconic to their particular  ability to contribute and I don't I  don't think we've really found the the  means of bringing out the best of  everyone for these vital overarching  issues so I don't want to only say that  it's got to be sort of an individual  leader but I think leaders can have the  capacity to inspire at every level all  the way down to the individual citizen  and leaders of that sort of think could  make a profound difference last thing I  want to touch on since you mentioned  these these strange times that we find  ourselves in what's your perspective on  what's going on with the corona virus  pandemic yeah I'll leave it wide open  whether you want to talk about curves or  whether you want to talk about  leadership or whatever the case is  where's your mind at this point on the  issue I mean there's so many things to  say but maybe just to stream line it one  thing that I see happening over and over  again and I'll speak to this from the  perspective of a mathematician yeah  scientist is the failure of so many  people to recognize what exponential  growth actually means and and and and  obviously what I mean by that is the the  point of exponential growth is that the  curve of growth starts out so slow that  at first you just don't think there's  any issue at all but then the curve  slams into you with such velocity in  such power that if you react to it  hitting you then it's just too late so  what that really means is the word over  reaction I think has a negative  connotation in everyday usage yes but in  this situation you have to overreact in  the sense that if you are only reacting  to the things that are literally  happening it's too late  you need to react to things that will  happen which means today you need to  overreact in order that you are prepared  for what's happening next and what I see  happening now is we're constantly just  reacting which means we're constantly  chasing after the curve right that seems  that applies to everything from saying  hey Mississippi only has a hundred cases  whoa now there's a thousand - we don't  really need this many ventilators right  now and then all of a sudden we do yeah  and you know - just to bring it down to  sort of a concrete individual level you  know I was on a book tour you know for  for this latest book and I wanted to  cancel various engagements so I'd speak  to the organizers and they'd say there's  no issue very few people and our  community are affected by this come  it'll be fine I'd be like no you don't  get it the point is when things are fine  that's when you need to act strongly so  that it stays fine nobody could get this  idea we've been speaking with Brian  green professor of physics and  mathematics also directs Columbia  University Center for Theoretical  Physics were linking to a number of his  many great books Brian I so appreciate  your time today thank you I enjoyed it
 

